WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.149
On cold, dark winter nights, you might catch
a glimpse of a horned figure stalking through

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:06.379
the streets.

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:11.559
With a basket strapped to its back and a switch
in hand, the demonic Krampus isn’t interested

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:16.020
in giving gifts but doling out brutal punishments.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.620
Krampus is the dark foil to St. Nicholas,
the miraculous Catholic bishop credited with

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.310
bringing small gifts of sweets, nuts, and
fruit to good little boys and girls in early

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:30.560
December (and yes, one of the inspirations
behind jolly old you-know-who).

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.180
But what are the origins of the demonic horned
creature?

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.270
You might be surprised to learn this winter
monster isn’t as old as you might have been

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.270
led to believe.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:45.190
And even more importantly—why invent a Yuletide
character that literally beats children into

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.550
submission?

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:52.270
[Monstrum Intro.]

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.829
The Krampus’s historical origins are generally
believed to have emerged onto the Yuletide

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:00.530
scene in Slavic and Austrian celebrations.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:05.829
Winter festivals in the Alpine regions of
Austria, Hungary, Slovenia, Croatia, Germany,

00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:11.659
and Italy all feature versions of this monstrous
anti-Santa, though originally “Krampus”

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:17.270
was really more of a category of monster rather
than the name of an individual creature.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:21.549
The word itself comes either from the Middle
German for ‘claw’ (Kralle) or the Bavarian

00:01:21.549 --> 00:01:26.530
word for something lifeless and dried out
(Krampn), with plenty of regional variations

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:27.709
[Kramperl, Klaubauf, Tuifl].

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:32.039
Half-goat, half-demon, Krampus are fanged,
with cloven feet and often long, pointed tongues.

00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:35.999
The oldest and most traditional versions have
multiple sets of horns.

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:40.170
They may carry a pitchfork and a basket to
collect children in.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.990
And they always have that switch in their
hand—most often one made of birch branches.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.899
The better to beat you with, my dear.

00:01:46.899 --> 00:01:53.349
Yep, that’s what this charming monster does—doles
out corporal punishment to children who misbehave.

00:01:53.349 --> 00:01:58.299
Krampus emerged in Alpine folklore alongside
celebrations of St. Nicholas.

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:03.340
At first it was more of an idea—an amorphous,
threatening boogeyman parents evoked to keep

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:09.080
kids in line when food was scarce and attention
wavered during the long dark nights of winter

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.429
in the mountains.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:15.540
Nice children received presents from St. Nick,
naughty children received a birch branch spanking

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.629
from Krampus, or worse.

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:22.379
The most wickedly behaved were shackled and
tossed into the monster’s wooden basket

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:24.579
to be carried away to the fires of Hell.

00:02:24.579 --> 00:02:27.900
Or, y’know, eaten by the Krampus.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.599
The traditions connecting Krampus and St.
Nicholas seem interwoven through time, despite

00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:36.209
some conflicting versions of how they came
to be associated.

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:40.629
There is evidence of performances that link
the Krampus to Satan in Jesuit theatre and

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:45.280
the monster frequently appears in St. Nicholas
plays, so it is possible that the beast was

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.620
constructed to represent the opposite of divinity.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:53.480
Detailed descriptions of Krampus only emerge
in the 19th century when folklorists began

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.970
recording the costumes worn during Krampuslauf
[Krampus-laouf], or “Krampus run.”

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:04.250
In the Alpine region, December 5 marks a celebratory—and
usually drunken—parade in which people dressed

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:09.840
as Krampus walk through the streets, scaring
bystanders, and chasing down the really unlucky

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.910
ones.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:15.090
Even if people dressed as Krampus don’t
actually injure people they are a symbolic

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.600
threat of violence and rebellion against decorum.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.470
In some regions, Krampus troops stalk the
streets from November to Christmas.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:27.329
These troops consist of multiple revelers,
and it's considered an honor, and often a

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:29.749
rite of passage to don the horns.

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:34.260
In the oldest traditions of the Alpine region,
Krampus revelers would either blacken their

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.709
faces, don a dark false beard, and dress in
dark clothing, or they wore wooden masks and

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:45.540
long fur coats, sporting belts with large
bells, and that switch in their hand, of course.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.310
In Catholic areas, where fear of the devil
was stronger, the costume included horns to

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.190
highlight their evil nature.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.640
​​Krampus masks are often called “Larven”
from the Latin word larva that means both

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.900
mask and ghost.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:04.969
Traditional masks are grotesque; with exaggerated
features and gaping mouths, accented by massive

00:04:04.969 --> 00:04:09.250
animal horns—usually multiple pairs from
different animals.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:14.260
The woodcarvers who craft these masks are
considered well-respected masters.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.101
Also in the mid-19th century, the monster
became associated with Santa Claus, the more

00:04:19.101 --> 00:04:22.090
secular version of St. Nicholas.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:25.070
Around this time is also where we see the
moniker ‘Krampus’ emerge.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:31.090
In 1862, a newspaper in Budapest was published
under Krampus’s name [Krampuszok ], and

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:37.430
in 1865 it published an image of Santa Claus
with Krampus lurking behind him, solidifying

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:41.470
the creature’s appearance and transferring
the connection to the Christian holiday of

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.760
Christmas rather than the holy saint’s feast
day.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.910
Claims that Krampus is derived from pagan
traditions intending to drive away winter

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:56.230
and evil spirits in an ancient fertility rite
are unfounded— in reality, there is no evidence

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.260
for this.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:01.670
Scholars found that the false pagan origin
story of Krampus was invented in the early

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:07.590
20th century by socialist folklorists attempting
to cast Christianity as a practice that sullied

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.350
ancient Nordic customs.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:14.340
The Nazi Party reinforced this false claim,
publishing a photo essay portraying the Krampus

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.550
as an “ancient custom” “banned for its
pagan roots.”

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:24.000
In reality, Krampus nights weren’t recorded
until 1582 in the Bavarian town of Diessen

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.860
in passing reference under the name Percht.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:31.520
The Perchten are mischievous Alpine spirits
and the likely folkloric precursors to the

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.520
Krampus.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:37.910
Documents from the 17th and 18th centuries
show Salzburg archbishops trying to ban the

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:40.900
Krampus tradition—with little success.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:45.620
Interestingly, these bans were not about the
monster itself but were aimed at curbing the

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.190
raucous celebrations that accompanied them.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:52.620
In the 19th century, the Krampuslauf tradition
dwindled, preserved only in a small number

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.680
of Austrian and German towns until the 1870s
as consumerism boomed.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.950
More specifically, red postcards with illustrations
of the monster appeared in Austria-Hungary

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.980
in the later-half of the century.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.220
Austria’s postal system became the first
to deliver postcards in 1867—making the

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:14.930
sending of Krampus cards in the late 1880s
possible, and financially viable.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:19.930
The cards became incredibly popular, a nostalgic
reminder for Austrians and Germans who moved

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.770
from rural areas where Krampus runs were popular,
to industrialized cities.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:29.120
Unsurprisingly, these postcards often show
the horned monster punishing children.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.430
Or, in a seemingly bizarre twist, accompanied
by semi-naked women.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.710
Perhaps because of these associations with
both youth and sexuality, in some parts of

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.540
Austria, children are told that being hit
with Krampus’s switch brings fertility.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:49.410
While globally Krampus’ popularity suffered
from the early-20th century associations with

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.830
Socialism and the Nazi Party, in the later
part of the century the demonic figure has

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.770
made a comeback.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.210
Since the turn of the 21st century, Krampus
events have become larger and more numerous

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:01.930
across the globe.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:05.740
Whether this is due to a renewed interest
in preserving traditional regional customs

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:11.630
as globalization emerges, or a rebellious
response to the over commodification and commercialization

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:16.580
of Christmas—the death metal version of
Santa is making a play to take back the winter

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:18.130
holidays.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:22.160
The Internet’s discovery of the 19th century
Krampus cards brought renewed interest to

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.620
the monster.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.430
In 2012 an episode of the Scooby-Doo!

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.870
Mystery Incorporated cartoon focused on the
Krampus.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:33.980
And that same year, Gerald Brom’s book Krampus:
the Yule Lord, further increased the popularity

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.730
of the monster—and connected him to a false
backstory in Nordic mythology and made him

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:40.130
an enemy of Santa Claus.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:45.380
Movies, books, and other popular culture texts
in America have perpetuated the connection

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.900
between Santa and Krampus.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:52.830
Horror films featuring the Krampus emerged
around 2010, and the monster has been a staple

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.160
ever since.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:00.060
The 2015 film Krampus plays on the Santa Claus/Krampus
connection explicitly—with Krampus coming

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:04.380
down chimneys, wearing a red fur-trimmed coat—and
slaughtering people.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:10.610
Commercialism, morbid fascination, reclamation—whatever
draws people to Krampus allows tradition to

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.110
be shared and preserved.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:18.920
In 2014, UNESCO even declared the Krampus-play
of the town of Oblarn in Styria as vital to

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.410
the cultural heritage of humanity.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:25.940
Which proves to me that it’s not only the
good guys that get immortalized—the monsters

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:28.610
get their place in history too.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:32.940
So this winter holiday season don’t forget
to play nice boys and girls—or you might

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:40.041
get a visit from this Yuletide monster.

